  "id": "tax.us.2025.rule.form1040-line25b",
  "notes": "Form 1040 line 25b: 'Add the amounts shown as federal income tax withheld' on Forms 1099-INT, 1099-DIV, 1099-R box 4, 1099-G box 4 and SSA-1099 box 6 (i1040gi-2025 l.2559-2576). Each document's own subtotal rule is pinned and added here, one addend per document, so the explanation walk reaches each one. Form 1099-K box 4 stays outside the class (SC-R1; a nonzero 1099-K box 4 is refused by the 1099-K loss-reason rule). v3 guarded the rule on every family's closure (require_closed). v4 adds, for each family, a count check beside line 25a's: the rule publishes only when the family holds at least as many box 4 (SSA-1099: box 6) answers as statements in the family that records the statement's income box (1099-INT box 1, 1099-DIV box 1a, 1099-R IRA box 1, 1099-G box 1, SSA-1099 box 5). A $0 answer is a recorded answer and counts; absence does not. Why: collect sums what is recorded and reads closure only for an empty set, so v3 published a smaller line 25b when a statement had no box 4 answer and the family's closure was attested true. Blocked as line 25a is: DEPENDENCY_ABSENT naming the family's withholding fact (the first family, in the order listed, that is short), and lines 25d and 33 through 37 wait behind it; lines 24 and 25a are unaffected. The check compares counts: it names the fact, not the statement, a statement recorded only through another box (1099-INT box 3, 8 or 10; 1099-DIV box 2a, 7 or 12) is not counted, and a surplus answer can offset a missing one. A return whose statements each carry their answer, including $0, computes as on v3.",
